PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B}

The scenario is governed by the Ontario Sale of Goods Act (RSO 1990, c S.1). Under s. 19, risk of loss passes with property unless the parties agree otherwise. Under Rule 2 of s. 20, where the seller must still put specific goods into a deliverable state, property (and therefore risk) does not pass until that work is completed and the buyer receives appropriate notice.  

Here the MacBook was not deliverable on 2 June because the screen still had to be replaced. Jake completed the repair and gave Luke timely notice on 5 June that the laptop would be ready for pickup the next day. At that point the goods were in a deliverable state and Luke had been notified, so risk passed to Luke before the flood. Jake therefore need not refund the purchase price.  

A is irrelevant (insurance is outside the question). C is incorrect because the notice was adequate. D states the wrong default rule (possession is not required once Rule 2 is satisfied). E is incorrect because the goods were not deliverable on 2 June.}

