PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The scenario involves a contract for the sale of specific goods under the Ontario Sale of Goods Act (RSO 1990, c S.1). Risk of loss is tied to passage of property/title (s. 21), which is governed by the statutory rules in s. 19. Rule 2 applies directly here: where the seller must perform an act to put specific goods into a deliverable state, property (and therefore risk) does not pass until that act is completed *and* the buyer receives notice that it has been done. 

On the facts, the MacBook Pro was not in a deliverable state on June 2 (it required a new screen). Jake completed the repair, but the only communication to Luke was a June 5 text stating the laptop was “almost finished” and would be ready the next day. That message is not notice that the goods *have* been put into a deliverable state. Consequently, property and risk remained with Jake when the flood occurred; Jake must refund the purchase price. 

A is irrelevant (insurance does not determine the parties’ rights inter se). B misstates the statutory notice requirement. D is incorrect because risk can pass before physical delivery. E is incorrect because the goods were not in a deliverable state on June 2.}

